
# Task 03 – Default Arguments and Function Overloading

## Aim
To understand default arguments and function overloading in C++.

## 1. Function Overloading
Function overloading allows multiple functions to have the same name with different parameter lists.

In this program, charge() is overloaded for:
- Integer weight in grams.
- Double weight in kilograms.
- Parcel objects.

## 2. Default Arguments
Default arguments provide predefined values when the caller does not supply all arguments.

Example:

double charge(double kg, bool express = false,
              bool insured = false)

Here:
- express defaults to false.
- insured defaults to false.

## 3. Ambiguous Function Call
The original design contains:

charge(double weight);
charge(double weight, bool express);
charge(double weight, bool express = false,
       bool insured = false);

Calling charge(2.0) can match all three functions because the extra parameters have defaults in the third function.

This creates ambiguity because the compiler cannot select one unique best overload.

The redesigned program removes the conflicting overloads.

## 4. Overload Resolution
The compiler considers:
1. Exact match.
2. Promotion.
3. Standard conversion.

An exact match is preferred over a conversion.

For example:
- charge(2) selects the integer overload.
- charge(2.0) selects the double overload.
- charge(2.0f) converts float to double.

## 5. Why Default Arguments Are Given from the Right
Default arguments must be specified from right to left.

Correct:
void display(int a, int b = 10, int c = 20);

Incorrect:
void display(int a = 10, int b, int c = 20);

A parameter without a default cannot follow one with a default in the same parameter list.

## 6. Alternative Concept
A single Parcel structure can hold all delivery options.

The program can accept one Parcel object containing weight, express and insurance information.

This makes it easier to add new options without creating many overloaded functions.

## 7. Complexity Analysis

Overload resolution:
Compile-time operation; no runtime overload-selection cost.

Charge calculation:
Time Complexity: O(1)

Additional parameter space:
O(1) for the supplied scalar arguments.

Passing a Parcel by const reference avoids copying the entire object.

## Result
The program demonstrates default arguments and function overloading. The redesigned functions avoid ambiguous calls and support different weight types and Parcel objects.
